 is, from the acquisition date, allocated to each of the Group’s Cash Generating Units (CGUs) or group of CGUs (refer to Note 20) that are expected to benefit from the combination, irrespective of whether other assets or liabilities of the acquiree are assigned to those units.","context":"Goodwill represents excess of the cost of acquisition over the Group’s share in the fair value of the acquiree’s identifiable assets, liabilities and contingent liabilities. If the excess is negative, a bargain purchase gain is recognized immediately in profit/(loss).","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2161,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"3cd4eb919d660ebae04093900fb2e6fd376eaf416a85542d4458bc25ed3522b2","quote":"Intangible assets acquired separately are measured on initial recognition at cost. The cost of intangible assets acquired in a business combination is their fair value at the date of acquisition. Following initial recognition, intangible assets are carried at cost less any accumulated amortization (calculated on a straight-line basis over their useful lives) and accumulated impairment losses, if any.","context":"Intangible assets","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2247,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"1e44a320fa0634e21640431e28c61f7324e0a6ed13191ac9e2531b50c0805baa","quote":"When the Group has transferred its rights to receive cash flows from an asset or has entered into a pass-through arrangement, it evaluates if, and to what extent, it has retained the risks and rewards of ownership.","context":"●The Group has transferred its rights to receive cash flows from the asset or has assumed an obligation to pay the received cash flows in full without material delay to a third party under a ‘pass-through’ arrangement; and either (a) the Group has transferred substantially all the risks and rewards of the asset, or (b) the Group has neither transferred nor retained substantially all the risks and rewards of the asset, but has transferred control of the asset.","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2318,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"3720c1a7d833b93392592d770624a07a40b37f974b0be0670cda0d8f16ac85f5","quote":"When the Group has transferred its rights to receive cash flows from an asset or has entered into a pass- through arrangement, it evaluates if, and to what extent, it has retained the risks and rewards of ownership. When it has neither transferred nor retained substantially all of the risks and rewards of the asset, nor transferred control of the asset, the Group continues to recognize the transferred asset to the extent of its continuing involvement. In that case, the Group also recognises an associated liability. The transferred asset and the associated liability are measured on a basis that reflects the rights and obligations that the Group has retained.","context":"When the Group has transferred its rights to receive cash flows from an asset or has entered into a pass-through arrangement, it evaluates if, and to what extent, it has retained the risks and rewards of ownership.","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2319,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"e84131d9588d4b85cff32f2beb1ffe2457a483d75bc661fd6734fdefbdc9e5f6","quote":"The useful lives of the Group’s intangible assets are determined by management at the time the asset is acquired based on historical experience, after considering market conditions, industry practice, technological developments, obsolescence and other factors. The useful life is reviewed by management periodically, including at each financial year end. The lives are based on historical experience with similar assets as well as anticipation of future events, which may impact their life, such as changes in technology.","context":"g)Useful life of Intangible assets","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2461,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"1242c08a2218069ffa5c925b0b53b409fdd6c1a0bac045db9167e7a47a9b481a","quote":"The Group previously held a 50% interest in Yatra MICE and Holidays Limited (formerly known as Yatra MICE and Holidays Private Limited) (“Yatra MICE”), an entity incorporated in India and engaged in the business of adventure travel and MICE services. The Group had entered into a Memorandum of Understanding (“MoU”) with Snow Leopard Adventures Private Limited (“SLA”) on September 28, 2012 to establish Yatra MICE as a joint venture entity.","context":"14. Investment in joint venture","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2589,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"7790fc5c1c86d942bd678ff663023e77e8e60299ecefd1e7f9fbdfb151f65408","quote":"Pursuant to a Share Purchase Agreement executed on June 19, 2024, the Group acquired an additional 49% of the equity share capital of Yatra MICE from SLA for a cash consideration of INR 9,799. Following the acquisition of the additional interest, the Group obtained control over Yatra MICE and accordingly Yatra MICE became a subsidiary of the Group from June 19, 2024. The Group remeasured its previously held interest in Yatra MICE at fair value and accounted for the acquisition in accordance with IFRS 3, Business Combinations.","context":"Prior to the acquisition of additional interest, the Group and SLA had joint control over Yatra MICE, with both parties having equal rights in the management of Yatra MICE and requiring unanimous consent for significant decisions at the board and shareholder levels. Accordingly, the Group accounted for its investment in Yatra MICE as a joint venture using the equity method in accordance with IAS 28, Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures.","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2591,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"d2f994855ce3c45d9fda8c858525051163b8135c4da029e48c956bd5cb37d08c","quote":"The assets acquired and liabilities assumed, and the resulting goodwill arising from the acquisition, have been disclosed in Note 43, Business combinations.","context":"Pursuant to a Share Purchase Agreement executed on June 19, 2024, the Group acquired an additional 49% of the equity share capital of Yatra MICE from SLA for a cash consideration of INR 9,799. Following the acquisition of the additional interest, the Group obtained control over Yatra MICE and accordingly Yatra MICE became a subsidiary of the Group from June 19, 2024. The Group remeasured its previously held interest in Yatra MICE at fair value and accounted for the acquisition in accordance with IFRS 3, Business Combinations.","section":"ITEM 19. EXHIBITS","block":2592,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"16aab7cad0e1fc2fec7d0b524c41fa75ffcc2d9c5d5a4a21d3c696ca50dbd2b3","quote":"Goodwill arose from business combinations completed in prior years and was initially allocated, from the acquisition date, to the respective acquired entities, representing the cash-generating units (“CGUs”) that were expected to benefit from the synergies arising from those business combinations. At the time of the respective acquisitions, these CGUs represented the lowest level within the Group at which the goodwill was monitored for internal management purposes and were not larger than the Group’s operating segments.","context":"Change in cash-generating units","section":"As Yatra MICE became a subsidiary during the year ended March 31, 2025, the Group’s consolidated financial statements include Yatra MICE’s results of operations from the date control was obtained.","block":2685,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"6edcbe6c95cbd8f5c57be902075ca01def2cc5fc10f08ce40d4df60a382378b7","quote":"Consequently, goodwill previously allocated to the individual acquired entities was reallocated to the revised CGUs using a relative value approach in accordance with IAS 36. Following the reallocation, goodwill is allocated to, and tested annually for impairment at, the following CGUs:","context":"Although Globe and TSI continue to operate as separate legal entities and were not part of the amalgamation, their operations are managed and monitored together with the Group’s respective business verticals. Accordingly, the legal entity structure was not considered determinative in identifying the Group’s CGUs for the purposes of impairment testing under IAS 36.","section":"As Yatra MICE became a subsidiary during the year ended March 31, 2025, the Group’s consolidated financial statements include Yatra MICE’s results of operations from the date control was obtained.","block":2695,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"7dad217c45cd9b56a75f0c65be5f691ae1eadd5d2a82a98d93d2bc3faeda36ea","quote":"In the year ended March 31, 2025, goodwill was tested for impairment at the level of the acquired subsidiaries, which represented the cash-generating units (“CGUs”) expected to benefit from the synergies arising from the respective business combinations. The recoverable amount of each CGU was determined using the value in use (“VIU”) methodology.","context":"(Amounts in INR thousands, except per share data and number of shares)","section":"As Yatra MICE became a subsidiary during the year ended March 31, 2025, the Group’s consolidated financial statements include Yatra MICE’s results of operations from the date control was obtained.","block":2726,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}}],"omittedEvidenceCount":9},"riskFactors":{"status":"no_evidence","evidence":[],"omittedEvidenceCount":0},"segmentIntelligence":{"status":"evidence_available","evidence":[{"id":"66c74c2189e78c6bcb44fb6240bce15b251e02b8b0ce9ea94474a1c9d8317c83","quote":"Our Consolidated Statement of Profit/(loss) and Other Comprehensive Income/(Loss) reports customer inducement costs as a reduction of revenue in the respective revenue lines. As certain parts of our revenues are recognized on a “net” basis when we are acting as an agent, and other parts of our revenue are recognized on a “gross” basis when we are acting as the principal, we evaluate our financial performance in each of our reportable segments based on Adjusted Margin, which is a non-IFRS measure and a segment profitability measure, as we believe that Adjusted Margin reflects the value addition of the travel services that we provide to our customers. Income from packages (including Meeting, Incentives, Conferences, & Exhibitions (“MICE”)), including income on airline tickets sold to customers as a part of tours and packages is accounted for on a “gross” basis as the Company controls the services before such services are transferred to travelers. Revenue from the packages business (including MICE), which is accounted for on a “gross” basis, represents the total amount paid by customers for these travel services and products, while our cost of procuring the relevant services and products for sale to our customers in this business is classified as service cost. See “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospects.”","context":"We evaluate our financial performance in each of our reportable segments based on our key performance indicators, Adjusted Margin and Adjusted Margin %, which are non-IFRS measures and segment profitability measures. Adjusted Margin represents IFRS revenue after adding back customer inducement costs in the nature of customer incentives, customer acquisition costs and loyalty program costs, which are reported as a reduction of revenue, and deducting the cost of acquisition of services primarily relating to sales to customers where we act as the principal. Adjusted Margin % represents Adjusted Margin as a percentage of gross bookings.","section":"","block":77,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"988c73bdda3c84f06ee0cee5d71ac6e9aea92b3330012bde237c855c5c497aa7","quote":"Our growth strategy involves capitalizing on the growth in the travel industry, expanding our hotels and holiday packages segment and railway ticketing operations, focusing on Tier 2 and Tier 3 cities, and strengthening our presence among corporates, investing in technology and enhancing cross-selling opportunities and promoting our brand. Our success in implementing our growth strategies is affected by:","context":"We may not be successful in implementing our growth strategies, which could adversely affect our business operations, financial condition, and cash flows.","section":"Airlines suppliers may move to a single GDS service provider platform for their domestic inventory and this could adversely affect our business and results of operations.","block":210,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"fef3ef3676669aa783789963ecf21cdba7d226b49a67e2d0a8b7474d35d1e50a","quote":"The Competition Act, 2002, as amended, (“Competition Act”), regulates practices that could have an appreciable adverse effect on competition in India. Under the Competition Act, any arrangement, understanding or action, whether formal or informal, which causes or is likely to cause an appreciable adverse effect on competition in India is void and may result in substantial penalties and compensation to be paid to persons shown to have suffered losses. Any agreement among competitors which directly or indirectly determines purchase or sale prices, results in bid rigging or collusive bidding, limits or controls production, supply, markets, technical development, investment or the provision of services, or shares the market or source of production or provision of services in any manner, including by way of allocation of geographical area or types of goods or services or number of customers in the market, is presumed to have an appreciable adverse effect on competition. Further, the Competition Act prohibits the abuse of a dominant position by any enterprise either directly or indirectly, including by way of unfair or discriminatory pricing or conditions in the sale of goods or services, using a dominant position in one relevant market to enter into, or protect, another relevant market, or to deny market access, and such practices are subject to substantial penalties and may also be subject to compensation for losses and orders to divide the enterprise. Further, the Competition Commission of India has extraterritorial powers and can investigate any agreements, abusive conduct or combination occurring outside India if such agreement, conduct, or combination has an appreciable adverse effect on competition in India.","context":"Our business and activities are regulated by the Competition Act, 2002, as amended.","section":"Business Connection.","block":434,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"ee13d4079f867078ba136cb09625bd9aa70f7b6053be117a3c9eeae5f087b32c","quote":"We believe India is one of the world’s largest and fastest growing economies, with a large middle class, increasing disposable income and a rapidly growing online consumer segment. As per the Reserve Bank of India, India’s GDP growth came in at 7.6% for fiscal year 2026 versus 6.5% for fiscal year 2025 and 8.2% in fiscal year 2024. The Reserve Bank of India is forecasting GDP growth of 6.9% for fiscal year 2027. Given the size and growth dynamics of the India travel market, we have strategically focused both on the corporate and consumer markets. We are the leading corporate travel service provider in India with over 1,340 large corporate customers and approximately 60,750 registered small and medium-sized (“SME”) customers as of March 31, 2026 and the second-largest player in the TMC and corporate OTA segment in the country in terms of market share for fiscal year 2024 (Videc report).","context":"Leisure and business travelers use our mobile applications, our website, www.yatra.com, and our other offerings and services to explore, research, compare prices and book a wide range of travel-related services. These services include domestic and international air ticketing on nearly all Indian and international airlines, as well as bus ticketing, rail ticketing, cab bookings and ancillary services within India. We also provide access through our platform to hotels, homestays, and other accommodations, with approximately 81,500 hotels and homestays in approximately 1,550 cities and towns across India and more than 2.9 million hotels around the world. To ensure that our service is truly a “one-stop shop” for travelers, we also provide our customers with access to approximately 850 holiday packages.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":598,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"24d291693fbec4550d02c041bae7d0ec2cc94c596bed5f7340a1a5d7211491fa","quote":"44","context":"We believe India is one of the world’s largest and fastest growing economies, with a large middle class, increasing disposable income and a rapidly growing online consumer segment. As per the Reserve Bank of India, India’s GDP growth came in at 7.6% for fiscal year 2026 versus 6.5% for fiscal year 2025 and 8.2% in fiscal year 2024. The Reserve Bank of India is forecasting GDP growth of 6.9% for fiscal year 2027. Given the size and growth dynamics of the India travel market, we have strategically focused both on the corporate and consumer markets. We are the leading corporate travel service provider in India with over 1,340 large corporate customers and approximately 60,750 registered small and medium-sized (“SME”) customers as of March 31, 2026 and the second-largest player in the TMC and corporate OTA segment in the country in terms of market share for fiscal year 2024 (Videc report).","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":599,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"faf5c686d604cdd0590c6b02aecbe163f6171e7045d9618683ac796dae5e02e2","quote":"The 13.6% year-over-year increased in Gross Bookings during fiscal 2026 was primarily attributable to softness in the air ticketing segment. Adjusted Margin also increased by 14% over the same fiscal year.","context":"Our revenue from rendering of services was INR 3,583.8 million, INR 7,383.5 million and INR 9,539.2 million in fiscal years 2024, 2025 and 2026, respectively. Adjusted Margin totaled INR 5,821.6 million in fiscal 2024, INR 5,373.9 million in fiscal 2025 and INR 6,462.9 million in fiscal 2026. Gross Bookings across for Air Ticketing, Hotels and Packages (including MICE) and other services businesses was INR 75,948.0 million, INR 70,910.2 million and INR 80,535.8 million in fiscal years 2024, 2025 and 2026 respectively. We reported net loss INR 366.5 million and net profit INR 23.5 million in fiscal years 2024 and 2025 respectively. In fiscal year 2026 we incurred a net loss of INR 66.0 million.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":615,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"588e63c5303d3517a05f2ff45fcf919779a1c514c9c2885f4d0c5e353e51dcc7","quote":"In our Air Ticketing Business Gross Bookings were INR 64,950.1 million, INR 55,272.8 million and INR 61,874.8 million for fiscal years 2024, 2025 and 2026 respectively. Corresponding Adjusted Margins were INR 4,502.4 million, INR 3,588.2 million and INR 4,372.7 million. In contrast, Gross Bookings from Hotels and Packages (including MICE) Business increased from INR 8,758.9 million for fiscal year 2024 to INR 13,053.4 million in fiscal year 2025 and further to INR 16,577.6 million in fiscal year 2026, while Adjusted Margin for this segment were INR 1,140.1 million, INR 1,472.7 million and INR 1,761.9 million respectively reflecting strong growth in the higher-margin leisure travel category.","context":"The 13.6% year-over-year increased in Gross Bookings during fiscal 2026 was primarily attributable to softness in the air ticketing segment. Adjusted Margin also increased by 14% over the same fiscal year.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":616,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"b1bf6923289eb920bf9d70d3d7859da5fe77b2a7d20eb07b88e9258ffd4bd0b3","quote":"The increase in revenue from services in fiscal 2026—up 129.2% from INR 7383.5 million in fiscal year 2025 to INR 9,539.2 million was driven by the rebound in travel demand and the continued scaling of our platform. However, the increase in Adjusted Margin during the same fiscal year was a result of competitive pricing pressures in air ticketing and increased promotional spending in the hotels and packages segment.","context":"In our Air Ticketing Business Gross Bookings were INR 64,950.1 million, INR 55,272.8 million and INR 61,874.8 million for fiscal years 2024, 2025 and 2026 respectively. Corresponding Adjusted Margins were INR 4,502.4 million, INR 3,588.2 million and INR 4,372.7 million. In contrast, Gross Bookings from Hotels and Packages (including MICE) Business increased from INR 8,758.9 million for fiscal year 2024 to INR 13,053.4 million in fiscal year 2025 and further to INR 16,577.6 million in fiscal year 2026, while Adjusted Margin for this segment were INR 1,140.1 million, INR 1,472.7 million and INR 1,761.9 million respectively reflecting strong growth in the higher-margin leisure travel category.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":617,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"7ec4394716e0232dc8a685a29fdb38b09e4af63e2c789b03dedf44e4b270e67c","quote":"Adjusted Margin is a non-IFRS measure. For more information about the non-IFRS measures and a reconciliation to the most comparable IFRS measure, please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospects-Key Operating Metrics.”","context":"The increase in revenue from services in fiscal 2026—up 129.2% from INR 7383.5 million in fiscal year 2025 to INR 9,539.2 million was driven by the rebound in travel demand and the continued scaling of our platform. However, the increase in Adjusted Margin during the same fiscal year was a result of competitive pricing pressures in air ticketing and increased promotional spending in the hotels and packages segment.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":618,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"38c7f3ac787f5284fc9d393887d1df30d6e32325d8bae1fbcc069b52b149f547","quote":"We believe India is one of the world’s largest and fastest growing economies, with a large middle class, increasing disposable income and a rapidly growing online consumer segment. India’s GDP growth is expected to come in at 7.6% in fiscal year 2026 up from 6.5% in fiscal year 2025 down from 8.2% in fiscal year 2024.","context":"Our Approach","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":625,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"63950a4b0bf77db84c1e879ca7c462d2e99143b1be004c6fbf427da71bc80e1d","quote":"In order to effectively grow our business and serve the various segments of India’s growing middle class, we operate through two go-to-market strategies: B2C and B2B. By using a common technology platform, we believe we are able to effectively target India’s educated urban consumers and have multiple points of contact for marketing additional services to existing customers.","context":"We believe India is one of the world’s largest and fastest growing economies, with a large middle class, increasing disposable income and a rapidly growing online consumer segment. India’s GDP growth is expected to come in at 7.6% in fiscal year 2026 up from 6.5% in fiscal year 2025 down from 8.2% in fiscal year 2024.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":626,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"1cae236ebceda37a5b96f148d260ee735387cae3e33e67a0f7ec9edd0fc9d48d","quote":"● | Our consumer, or B2C, offerings are provided directly to consumers through our apps and website. | ● | Our corporate, or B2B, includes corporate travel and our travel agent business.","context":"In order to effectively grow our business and serve the various segments of India’s growing middle class, we operate through two go-to-market strategies: B2C and B2B. By using a common technology platform, we believe we are able to effectively target India’s educated urban consumers and have multiple points of contact for marketing additional services to existing customers.","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":627,"table":"● | Our consumer, or B2C, offerings are provided directly to consumers through our apps and website.\n | \n● | Our corporate, or B2B, includes corporate travel and our travel agent business.","attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"743172f158f9054e7a5333e2c87b1de9585aefd8d0e683cd7653335df7c169bb","quote":"● | Yatra: Our primary mobile interface to our core platform, which has been downloaded approximately 22.4 million times. | ● | Yatra Corporate: A self-booking application for our business customers.","context":"As smartphone penetration has grown in India, our mobile apps have become a critical component of our consumer offerings. We have multiple applications for a variety of consumer segments and services including:","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":653,"table":"● | Yatra: Our primary mobile interface to our core platform, which has been downloaded approximately 22.4 million times.\n | \n● | Yatra Corporate: A self-booking application for our business customers.","attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"dd53893e51c31261979e0f00584a18fb4ea497bedaafaa08a982b0cff6c1b448","quote":"With a strong presence in both B2B and B2C travel markets, Yatra continues to redefine industry benchmarks by integrating AI-driven recommendation engines, intuitive booking systems, and real-time customer support infrastructure. The YouGov ranking serves as third-party validation of Yatra’s strategy to lead with innovation and customer satisfaction.","context":"Innovation-Led Growth Across Segments","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":687,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"9aa852b5c5fcabd80f02a3b88d08c332fe9fe9619af9e7c7f552651becc56ae7","quote":"The airline ticketing business is primarily targeted to domestic and international air passengers from India. We have access to real-time inventory either via GDS service providers such as Amadeus and Galileo or through a “direct-connect” to the airlines. We have relationships with all major airlines operating in India, domestic and international. The fares paid by our customers include a transaction fee and this, along with the “per-segment” earnings from the GDS providers, the commissions and volume-linked incentives from the airlines, forms the revenue accrued to us. We provide our customers with access to over 400 airlines for domestic and international travel. Our relationships include all major full-service carriers and low-cost carriers. These include domestic carriers such as Air India, Air India Express, Akasa Air, IndiGo, SpiceJet, Vistara (now merged with Air India), Alliance Air, and international airlines such as Air France-KLM, British Airways, Emirates, Etihad Airways, Lufthansa, Malaysia Airlines, Singapore Airlines, Thai Airways and Qatar Airways, etc.","context":"Air Ticketing Business","section":"ITEM 4. INFORMATION ON THE COMPANY","block":694,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"22d040af9a5ac12ea35cff40698e817877bd10248990c93e3f34d16e9e4d4308","quote":"Gross Bookings for our Hotels and Holiday Packages segment increased by 27.0% from fiscal year 2025 to fiscal year 2026, primarily driven by growth in hotel room-night sales. However, the recovery in holiday package sales remained subdued, reflecting ongoing constraints on international travel during a substantial portion of the fiscal year.","context":"Revenue from our Hotels business comprises of commissions and markups which are earned from the sale of standalone hotel rooms and are recognized on a net basis. In contrast, revenue from holiday packages (including MICE), which comprise of both hotel accommodations and airline tickets, are recognized on a gross basis.","section":"(1) | Data for flagship brand Yatra.com only. (2) | Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospects-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":706,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"44ad09d719e5a3d7b2f2c9913f0caab367a05b70033dba563e320b66a597b42a","quote":"54","context":"Gross Bookings for our Hotels and Holiday Packages segment increased by 27.0% from fiscal year 2025 to fiscal year 2026, primarily driven by growth in hotel room-night sales. However, the recovery in holiday package sales remained subdued, reflecting ongoing constraints on international travel during a substantial portion of the fiscal year.","section":"(1) | Data for flagship brand Yatra.com only. (2) | Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospects-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":707,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"7fe90dde589399c4fe3d7bde663a845210dc5fe245402ccf360dfee8bb61f75b","quote":"(1) | Management estimates, as of March 31, 2026. (2) | Includes approximately 3,700 homestay accommodations. (3) | Premium hotels include 4- and 5-star accommodations, mid-segment hotels include 3-star accommodations and budget hotels include all other accommodations (including homestay accommodations).","context":"Yatra has one of India’s largest hotel inventory with approximately 81,500 properties on our platform, especially in the key “budget” category in Tier 2 and Tier 3 cities that matches Indian consumers’ preferences.","section":"(1) | Data for flagship brand Yatra.com only. (2) | Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospects-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":710,"table":"(1) | Management estimates, as of March 31, 2026.\n(2) | Includes approximately 3,700 homestay accommodations.\n(3) | Premium hotels include 4- and 5-star accommodations, mid-segment hotels include 3-star accommodations and budget hotels include all other accommodations (including homestay accommodations).","attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"ddd5ef4bb9347bc627e1d9f2420efa8757e9ed0678bafa5a54f8c4c4e758719a","quote":"We have designed a unique “go-to-market” strategy that is a mix of B2C and B2B. This comprehensive approach creates strong network effects resulting in significant cross-sell between business and leisure travelers, which we believe addresses the entire travel market in India. We are India’s second-largest player in the TMC and corporate OTA segment in the country in terms of market share for fiscal year 2024. (Source: Videc Report). We believe that our broad and diverse offerings provide us with considerable cross-selling opportunities across business channels and our eCash program furthers customer loyalty across channels, builds a diversified customer base. These channels enable us to provide end-to-end travel solutions for passengers travelling domestically and internationally.","context":"Our Synergistic Multi-Channel Go to Market approach for Business and Leisure Travelers","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":742,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"f0695acd7dcbac2375801e506782e22447dd93de73e3f9012e080b29ce6f7d46","quote":"B2C Channel. Our B2C distribution channel commenced operations in 2006 and focuses on the growing Indian middle-class population and their increasing travel requirements and provides them with travel products and services through our websites and mobile applications. The largest category of customers for online travel agencies is the B2C segment, i.e., direct or retail customers, that use online platforms for bookings, who accounted for approximately 78% of the overall online travel agencies’ net revenue in fiscal year 2024. (Source: Videc Report).","context":"We have designed a unique “go-to-market” strategy that is a mix of B2C and B2B. This comprehensive approach creates strong network effects resulting in significant cross-sell between business and leisure travelers, which we believe addresses the entire travel market in India. We are India’s second-largest player in the TMC and corporate OTA segment in the country in terms of market share for fiscal year 2024. (Source: Videc Report). We believe that our broad and diverse offerings provide us with considerable cross-selling opportunities across business channels and our eCash program furthers customer loyalty across channels, builds a diversified customer base. These channels enable us to provide end-to-end travel solutions for passengers travelling domestically and internationally.","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":743,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"4f49d3f3ce3c0345a470f2c2020ae4b2dd1f6fee00dc79263bb1c3bca1ebe663","quote":"B2B Channel. Our B2B channel includes corporate travel and our travel agent business.","context":"B2C Channel. Our B2C distribution channel commenced operations in 2006 and focuses on the growing Indian middle-class population and their increasing travel requirements and provides them with travel products and services through our websites and mobile applications. The largest category of customers for online travel agencies is the B2C segment, i.e., direct or retail customers, that use online platforms for bookings, who accounted for approximately 78% of the overall online travel agencies’ net revenue in fiscal year 2024. (Source: Videc Report).","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":744,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"99e631783abeb4be48ba31c88ce03ce578a279d48fe85269fd0819ae6f87ad21","quote":"SME customers. The corporate travel segment in the Indian TMC & OTA market is expected to grow at CAGR of 15% from 2024 to 2027 (Videc report).","context":"(*The 78% figure is based on a total $14.5 billion OTA market in 2024, where $3 billion is attributed to corporate travel through OTA/TMCs, thus remaining $ 11.5 billion will be non-corporate (“B2C”) travel.)","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":747,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"470242b1b8064b98d445c68b15a537e16dd08b8120b6cfc4f619c4fb0511741e","quote":"Travel Agents: This business segment helps us to reach customers in smaller markets (Tier 2 and Tier 3 cities) where Internet penetration has traditionally been lower. It provides additional scale to our business by leveraging our technology platform in order to cost-effectively aggregate consumer demand from travel agents located across India. As of March 31, 2026, we had approximately 55,800 travel agents registered with us across most major cities in India. Our network of travel agents has allowed us to expand our footprint and distribution network in India.","context":"We have further expanded our corporate travel go-to-market strategy beyond large enterprises to serve medium-sized enterprises through a combination of direct sales and digital onboarding. We have also strengthened our offerings through our MICE business and the launch of our integrated Expense Management Solution, enabling customers to manage travel booking, expense capture, approvals and reimbursements through a unified platform. We believe these initiatives strengthen customer engagement, increase wallet share and position us to address a broader portion of enterprise travel and expense spend","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":749,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"4d15ff3541e7f3239291d41d8688f7b9affc3d253e2af7d274375610f3b9b1dc","quote":"In addition to our travel agent network, we have expanded our distribution through API integrations with online and offline travel partners, extending our reach into Tier 2 and Tier 3 markets while generating incremental demand on our platform. We believe this distribution channel provides a scalable avenue for future growth, particularly as we continue to expand our hotel supply and drive greater adoption across this partner ecosystem.","context":"Travel Agents: This business segment helps us to reach customers in smaller markets (Tier 2 and Tier 3 cities) where Internet penetration has traditionally been lower. It provides additional scale to our business by leveraging our technology platform in order to cost-effectively aggregate consumer demand from travel agents located across India. As of March 31, 2026, we had approximately 55,800 travel agents registered with us across most major cities in India. Our network of travel agents has allowed us to expand our footprint and distribution network in India.","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":750,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"f2c9823b7457e61cc09e320933bb51e079b61afb2c205a7a78c6fe8b20b13e71","quote":"We aim to expand our customer base by continuing to offer both business and leisure travelers a seamless, integrated technology platform that addresses their end-to-end travel requirements. Our corporate clients collectively employ over 9 million individuals, whom we consider part of a large and growing consumer segment with meaningful disposable income.","context":"Growth in the customer base using cost-effective technology solutions","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":782,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"b51688733b7635e851747f821d5beda4a83eef456de18020ab514bc8544b2653","quote":"To capture this opportunity, we have launched a loyalty program designed to incentivize employees who book through our corporate travel platform to also use our B2C platform for their personal travel needs. This cross-utilization strategy allows us to acquire and engage customers in a cost-efficient manner while driving higher average revenue per user (ARPU).","context":"We aim to expand our customer base by continuing to offer both business and leisure travelers a seamless, integrated technology platform that addresses their end-to-end travel requirements. Our corporate clients collectively employ over 9 million individuals, whom we consider part of a large and growing consumer segment with meaningful disposable income.","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":783,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"a957de0c430621ccd9f92945b088f9907fd40ea2ecc1bbd56230c8ebc10dd9bc","quote":"The acquisition of companies, intellectual property and talented individuals has been central to our growth strategy. In 2010, we acquired TSI and its subsidiaries in order to expand our travel agent business, particularly our international Air Ticketing Business for small and medium scale enterprises. In 2012, we acquired the Travelguru (merged with Yatra India) group of companies from Travelocity, which remain well-established hotel aggregators in India. Through this acquisition, we expanded our hotel business by establishing more direct hotel relationships in India and improved our inventory of affordable travel options. We have also leveraged our leading position in the Indian travel ecosystem to make several “acqui-hires,” including the teams from mGaadi and dudegenie, in order to grow our business. During the second quarter of fiscal year 2018 and fourth quarter of fiscal year 2019, we completed the acquisition of a majority stake in Yatra for Business Private Limited (formerly known as Air Travel Bureau Private Limited – merged with Yatra India) and the corporate division of PL Worldways known as Travel.co.in Private Limited (merged with Yatra India), or “TCIL”, which further reinforced our leadership position in the B2B travel segment. We completed the acquisition of GAISL on September 11, 2024. The GAISL integration has led to delivering synergies in supplier consolidation, cross-selling opportunities, and our ability to offer seamless, tech-driven solutions to our growing client base.","context":"Fuel Growth Through Innovative Acquisition Strategies","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":807,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"ddbfff0344597d76cd7aa78ec3a2a7bdb978d9cb5bd70f83e1ac9598fa14f3eb","quote":"As of March 31, 2026, we had 1,584 employees. The following tables show a breakdown of our employees as of March 31 for the past three years by category of activity and geographic location.","context":"Employees","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":844,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0001493152-26-035684","form":"20-F","filedAt":"2026-07-31","reportDate":"2026-03-31","cik":"0001516899","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1516899/000149315226035684/form20-f.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d.bin","sha256":"a4bc0d9bb00b77dd50a28dc24785e4fa76cd12a6474951570c567165f42f6d9d"}},{"id":"11055c8ab8d4a6fe709fd252a23569e6fb4717fb7abd9794f217148547dfa846","quote":"| | Number of Employees | | | as of March 31, | Division/Function | | 2024 | | | 2025 | | | 2026 | Executive Management | | | 3 | | | | 3 | | | | 4 | Product development | | | 7 | | | | 6 | | | | 13 | Sales and marketing | | | 260 | | | | 236 | | | | 355 | Technology development and technology support | | | 126 | | | | 136 | | | | 159 | Others (including operations, business development, administration, finance and accounting, legal and human resources) | | | 872 | | | | 1,071 | | | | 1,053 | Total | | | 1,268 | | | | 1,452 | | | | 1,584 |","context":"As of March 31, 2026, we had 1,584 employees. The following tables show a breakdown of our employees as of March 31 for the past three years by category of activity and geographic location.","section":"Please refer to “Item 5. Operating and Financial Review and Prospect-Key Operating Metrics” for definitions and reconciliations of non-IFRS measures.","block":845,"table":" |  | Number of Employees | \n |  | as of March 31, | \nDivision/Function |  | 2024 |  |  | 2025 |  |  | 2026 | \nExecutive Management |  |  | 3 |  |  |  | 3 |  |  |  | 4 | \nProduct development |